function curriculumChunk1_() {
  return {"grade6":{"g6-bridging-w1-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core","number":1,"week":1,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"Data Quality and AI Outputs","summary":"Students trace the causal chain from a specific data-quality problem to the specific output mistake it causes, and separate the quality of a dataset from its size.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU"],"objectives":["Explain, with an example, how higher- or lower-quality data changes an AI output.","Predict the effect on the output when the data quality is degraded or improved.","Distinguish the quality of a dataset from the quantity of data in it."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Data Quality and AI Outputs","body":["Imagine teaching a younger cousin to recognise dates using a photo album. If half the photos are blurry and one photo is actually a stone, incorrectly labelled as a date, what will your cousin get wrong? An AI learns the same way you would teach - from the examples it is shown. Today you become a data detective who can predict an AI's mistakes just by studying its data."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can explain, using a clear example, how the quality of data changes what an AI produces.","I can predict whether an output will get better or worse when the data is made cleaner or noisier, and say why.","I can tell the difference between the amount of data and the quality of data."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"data quality","definition":"How accurate, complete, consistent, and representative a dataset is."},{"term":"label","definition":"The name attached to an example that tells the model what it is."},{"term":"noise","definition":"Irrelevant or inconsistent detail in data that confuses the pattern."},{"term":"representative","definition":"The data covers the real range of cases the model will meet."},{"term":"causal chain","definition":"A step-by-step link from a cause to its effect."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"The model learns from its examples","body":["An AI model is not filled with rules that a person typed. Instead it studies a dataset and adjusts itself to match the patterns it finds.","This means the dataset is really the model's teacher. If the teacher shows wrong examples, the model learns the wrong lesson - and it does so confidently."],"role":"explain"},{"authored":true,"type":"diagram","variant":"flow","label":"How a data problem becomes an output mistake","heading":"One flaw, traced all the way to the answer","altText":"A chain of three boxes: a data problem leads to a wrong learned pattern, which leads to a wrong output.","caption":"This is the chain you trace on your worksheet. Start at the data, not the answer.","nodes":[{"label":"A problem in the data","detail":"one stone is labelled 'date'"},{"label":"The model learns it","detail":"small round = date"},{"label":"The output is wrong","accent":true,"detail":"it calls a pebble a date"}],"edges":[{"label":"so the model learns"},{"label":"so the output will"}],"role":"diagram"},{"type":"text","heading":"Quality is not the same as quantity","body":["It is tempting to think more data always helps. But a huge dataset full of mistakes just teaches the wrong pattern more strongly.","A small, clean, well-chosen dataset can beat a giant messy one. Before trusting any output, a careful builder checks the quality of the data behind it - and never puts personal or identifying information into a tool."],"role":"explain"},{"authored":true,"type":"reference","label":"Your dataset card","heading":"What a dataset card shows you","intro":"Your teacher gives you a card describing one dataset. Read it in this order — the flaw is never announced, you have to find it.","entries":[{"term":"The examples","asks":"How many items are there, and what is each one a picture of?","test":"A small set is not automatically a bad one."},{"term":"The labels","asks":"What is each example called? Is every label correct?","test":"One wrong label teaches one wrong lesson."},{"term":"The spread","asks":"Does the set cover the real range of cases, or only some of them?","test":"A gap is as damaging as an error."},{"term":"The noise","asks":"Is anything blurry, inconsistent or irrelevant?","test":"Noise makes the pattern harder to find."}],"role":"reference"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will study a dataset card, predict the exact mistake the AI will make, then watch the real outputs to check your detective work."],"items":["Read your dataset card and note how each item is labelled.","Decide the quality problem: error, gap, or noise.","Predict one exact output mistake and write it down before the reveal.","Compare your prediction with the revealed clean and degraded outputs.","Write one sentence linking the data cause to the output effect."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["When could a bigger dataset actually make an AI worse?","How can an output be wrong and still sound completely sure of itself?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one thing you can now predict about an AI just by looking at its data.","Name one place in daily life where poor-quality data could lead to an unfair result."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 1 - Core - Worksheet | Data Quality and AI Outputs","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Trace the chain: for your dataset card, fill each box: the data problem, the learned pattern problem, and the predicted output problem. Use an arrow between each.","stem":"The data problem is ___.\nSo the model learns ___.\nSo the output will ___.","lookFor":"All three links completed so the chain runs from a named data problem (an error, a gap or noise) through the pattern the model learns to one specific output error - for example inconsistent 'date'/'dat'/'DATE' labels and a stone labelled 'date', so the model reads a small stone as a date. Not yet: the output is said to be 'bad' with no data cause named.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Quality or quantity? Read four short scenarios and decide whether the dataset's problem is about quality or quantity. Justify each choice in one sentence.","stem":"This is a ___ problem because ___.","lookFor":"Separates volume from correctness: strong answers say that adding thousands more mislabelled examples would make the wrong pattern stronger, not weaker, so a bigger dataset can still be a worse dataset. Watch for the misconception that more data always makes an AI better.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Prediction vs reveal log: record your prediction before the reveal, then what the outputs actually showed, then whether you were right. Log each dataset version, your predicted output error, the actual revealed output, and Match? (Y/N) and why.","stem":"Dataset version ___ | My predicted output error ___ | Actual revealed output ___ | Match? (Y/N) and why ___","lookFor":"A specific predicted mistake tied to a specific bad example, written before the reveal, and an honest match judgement afterwards. Partial: the prediction records a general direction of change with weak justification.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: in one or two sentences, explain how making a dataset noisier would change an AI's output, and why.","lookFor":"Predicts a specific, correct change in the output when the data is degraded and justifies it - noise confuses the pattern so the model still answers confidently but gets more cases wrong. Not yet: cannot predict, or the prediction is unrelated to the data change.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): design your own dataset card with a hidden quality problem, swap with a partner, and see if they can predict the exact output mistake it would cause.","lookFor":"Builds a card whose flaw is real but hidden, and can state in advance the exact output error it should cause. Stretch further by asking for two overlapping quality problems and how the errors would combine.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-bridging-w1-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab","number":2,"week":1,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Data Quality Investigation (Lab)","summary":"Teams run a fair test across four versions of one dataset, record how each output shifts from the clean baseline, and rank the datasets with output evidence.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU"],"objectives":["Run a hands-on comparison of clean vs noisy data and record how the output changes.","Rank data sets by expected output quality and justify the order.","Use evidence from the comparison, rather than opinion, to explain the data-to-output link."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Data Quality Investigation (Lab)","body":["In the Core lesson you predicted one AI mistake from its data. Today you run a proper investigation: four versions of the same dataset, four sets of outputs, and one big question - which data quality problem does the most damage? By the end you will rank them and defend your order with evidence, just like a real AI auditor."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run a fair comparison of clean and noisy data and record what changes in the output.","I can rank several datasets from best to worst expected output and justify my ranking.","I can use evidence from the comparison to explain the data-to-output link."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"fair test","definition":"A comparison where you change only one thing so you can tell what caused a result."},{"term":"baseline","definition":"The clean result you compare all other versions against."},{"term":"evidence","definition":"A specific observation that supports a claim."},{"term":"ranking","definition":"Ordering items from best to worst using a clear reason."},{"term":"audit","definition":"A careful check of data or a system before you trust it."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Make the comparison fair","body":["To find out how data quality changes an output, you must be fair: keep the task and the model the same, and change only the dataset.","Then any difference you see in the output can be traced back to the data - that is what makes your evidence trustworthy."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Rank with evidence","body":["After you see how each dataset changed the output, you rank them from best to worst expected quality.","The hard part is the middle: when two datasets are both weak, you decide which flaw hurt the output more, and you point to the exact output change that proves it."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Your team compares four output sets against a clean baseline, records what changed, and ranks the datasets from best to worst with evidence."],"items":["Study the clean baseline output so you know what correct looks like.","For each degraded version, write exactly how the output changed.","Match each change to its data flaw.","Score each dataset on accuracy, completeness, and consistency.","Place all four on the ranking line and justify the order."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which data flaw did the most damage, and what evidence proves it?","When two datasets are both weak, how did your team decide which was worse?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one output change that most convinced you of your top ranking.","Describe how you would audit a dataset before trusting an AI built on it."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 1 - Lab - Worksheet | Data Quality Investigation (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Change from baseline: for each degraded version, describe exactly how the output changed from the clean baseline, and name the data flaw responsible.","stem":"Compared to the baseline, this output ___.\nThe flaw responsible is ___.","lookFor":"A specific output difference tied to a specific flaw - for example, the error version confidently mislabels some items because its training labels were wrong, so it repeats the wrong label as if it were correct. Partial: some changes recorded but the link to the flaw is vague or a version is missed.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Defend the ranking: write your full best-to-worst ranking and justify the two placements you found hardest to separate, using output evidence.","stem":"My order is ___.\nThe hardest pair was ___ because the output ___.","lookFor":"A defensible full ranking justified with output evidence, including the hardest pair - a model order is clean best, then noise, then gaps, then error worst, because wrong labels teach an actively false pattern while noise only adds confusion. Accept other orders if the justification cites output evidence; if a ranking has no evidence, prompt 'point to the output change that proves it'.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Investigation log: record each dataset version, its output change, its data flaw, and its dimension scores.","stem":"Version ___ | Output change vs baseline ___ | Data flaw ___ | Accuracy/Complete/Consistent score ___","lookFor":"Specific, accurate output changes recorded for every version, each linked to its data flaw, with the three dimensions scored. Not yet: vague impressions with no link to a data flaw.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: which single data flaw would you fix first to improve the output the most, and why?","lookFor":"Names one flaw from their own log and justifies it from the output evidence they gathered, rather than from opinion or a general statement about data quality.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): invent a fifth dataset that combines two flaws. Predict where it lands on the ranking line and justify your prediction with the evidence you gathered.","lookFor":"Weighs two flaws against each other and places the combined dataset using evidence from the four-way comparison rather than a hunch.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-bridging-w2-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core","number":3,"week":2,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"Refining Prompts for Better Outputs","summary":"Students learn a refinement loop - set the goal, judge the output, name one weakness, make a single targeted change, and re-judge - so they can say exactly why a prompt change improved the result.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["SD"],"objectives":["Improve a weak prompt so the output better meets the goal.","Explain which change improved the output and why it worked."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["You ask an AI to 'write about Al Ain' and get a dull, general paragraph. Was the AI wrong - or was the prompt?","Today you become a prompt engineer who sets a clear goal, judges the output against it, and makes one smart change at a time. Best of all, you will be able to say exactly why your change worked."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can turn a weak prompt into a stronger one that better meets the goal.","I can name the exact change I made and explain why it improved the output.","I can judge an output against the goal instead of just accepting it."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"prompt","definition":"The instruction and context you give an AI to guide its output."},{"term":"goal","definition":"What you want the output to achieve, stated clearly."},{"term":"constraint","definition":"A limit you set, such as length, tone, or format."},{"term":"ambiguous","definition":"Unclear or open to more than one meaning."},{"term":"refinement loop","definition":"Goal, judge, name the weakness, change, re-judge - repeated."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Judge before you change","body":["You cannot improve an output until you judge it honestly against the [[goal]]. Ask: what is missing, and what is off-target?","Common weaknesses in a prompt are a missing audience, missing format, missing [[constraint]], or an [[ambiguous]] task. Naming the weakness tells you exactly what to fix."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Change one thing, and know why","body":["Strong refiners change **one thing at a time** and predict why it should help before they test it. That way, when the output improves, they know which change did it.","Specificity comes from the task, the audience, and the format - never from personal or private information. A precise [[prompt]] is not a personal one."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will take a weak prompt with a clear goal, name one weakness, write a targeted v2, predict its effect, and check against the revealed output."],"items":["Read the weak prompt and its goal.","Judge the v1 output and name one weakness.","Write a v2 that targets only that weakness.","Predict how the output should change and why.","Compare with the revealed v2 output and log it."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Why is changing one thing at a time smarter than changing many?","How can you make a prompt specific without adding any private information?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one change that most improved your output and why it worked.","Describe a time a clearer instruction would have helped you get a better result in real life."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 2 - Core - Worksheet | Refining Prompts for Better Outputs","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"For the weak prompt given, name one weakness and write a v2 that targets only that weakness.","stem":"The weakness is ___.\nMy v2 prompt is ___.","lookFor":"Names a weakness that genuinely matches the output problem (ambiguous task, or missing audience, format, or constraint), and a v2 that directly targets that one named weakness rather than bundling several edits or simply lengthening the prompt.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"After the reveal, explain the causal reason your change improved the output.","stem":"My change was ___.\nIt improved the output because ___.","lookFor":"A causal explanation linking the specific change to the improvement (e.g. naming the audience and length removed the model's need to guess, so the output became focused and age-appropriate). Reward causal reasoning, not 'it is better'.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Record your v1, the weakness, your v2 change, the predicted effect, and the observed effect.","stem":"v1 prompt | Weakness named | v2 change | Predicted vs observed effect","lookFor":"A usable log in which one change is recorded per row with a stated reason, so the improvement can be attributed to a cause; predicted and observed effects are both recorded and compared, not just asserted.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"Explain in one or two sentences which prompt change improved your output and the reason it worked.","stem":"","lookFor":"Names one specific change and gives a clear causal reason for the improvement. Partial: a general reason with a weak causal link. Not yet: states it is better with no reason.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Write a v3 that improves the output on a second goal (for example, making it both clearer and more accurate) without losing the first improvement.","stem":"","lookFor":"A v3 that adds a gain on a second dimension while preserving the first gain, with reasoning that shows awareness that the two goals must both be met rather than traded away.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]}]}},"g6-bridging-w2-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab","number":4,"week":2,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Prompt Refinement Workshop (Lab)","summary":"Teams iterate a weak prompt across several versions with a one-change-per-version log, then refine for clarity and accuracy together and justify a best-balanced version from their trade-off evidence.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["SD"],"objectives":["Iterate a prompt across several versions to raise output quality, logging each change and its effect.","Refine for two goals at once (clarity and accuracy) and discuss the trade-off."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["In the Core lesson you made one smart change to a prompt. Today you run a full workshop: version 1, 2, 3, maybe 4 - each a single change you can defend.","Then comes the real challenge of a designer: chasing two goals at once, clarity and accuracy, and discovering that helping one can cost the other. Your log will prove exactly what worked."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can iterate a prompt across several versions and log each change and its effect.","I can refine for two goals at once and explain the trade-off between them.","I can use my log as evidence for which change helped the most."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"iterate","definition":"To improve something step by step across several versions."},{"term":"trade-off","definition":"When improving one goal makes another goal worse."},{"term":"balance","definition":"The best compromise between competing goals for a purpose."},{"term":"accuracy","definition":"How correct and on-topic the output is."},{"term":"clarity","definition":"How easy the output is to understand."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Iterate and keep the receipts","body":["A designer does not stop at the first improvement. You [[iterate]] - version by version - changing one thing each time and logging its effect.","The log is your **evidence**. When someone asks which change helped most, you point to the row that proves it, because you changed only one thing that version."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Two goals, one trade-off","body":["Now aim for two goals at once: [[clarity]] and [[accuracy]]. Sometimes a change that makes the output clearer quietly drops an accurate detail.","That is a [[trade-off]]. Your job is not to pretend it away but to choose the version that best balances both goals for your purpose - and to say why."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Your team iterates a weak prompt across several versions, logging each change and effect, then balances clarity and accuracy and chooses the best version with evidence."],"items":["Set the goal and judge the v1 output.","Make v2 with one change; log the reason and revealed effect.","Iterate to v3 and v4, one change each, logging cause and effect.","Add accuracy as a second goal alongside clarity.","Find and record a trade-off where one goal cost the other.","Choose the best-balanced version and justify it from your log."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which single change gave the biggest quality jump, and how does your log prove it?","Where did clarity and accuracy pull against each other, and how did you decide?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the best-balanced version your team chose and one sentence on why.","Explain what balancing two goals taught you about making design decisions."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 2 - Lab - Worksheet | Prompt Refinement Workshop (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"For each version, write the one change you made, your reason, and the effect on the output.","stem":"Version ___ change: ___.\nReason: ___.\nEffect: ___.","lookFor":"One single change per version with a clear cause-and-effect entry (e.g. v2 added the audience 'Grade 3'; reason: remove guessing; effect: output became simpler and on-level). Watch for entries that bundle several edits or that only lengthen the prompt with no reason.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Describe one point where improving clarity hurt accuracy (or the reverse), then choose your best-balanced version and justify it.","stem":"Improving ___ hurt ___ when I ___.\nMy best-balanced version is ___ because ___.","lookFor":"A specific place where one goal cost the other (e.g. cutting the notice to two sentences for clarity dropped the accurate event time), plus a best-balanced version justified from the log against the purpose. Accept any version if justified; the last version is not automatically best.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Record each version's change, reason, clarity effect, and accuracy effect.","stem":"Version | Single change | Reason | Effect on clarity / accuracy","lookFor":"Several versions logged, each with one change and a clear cause-effect entry across both goals. Quality of reasoning per row matters more than the number of rows.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"Which version was your best balance of clarity and accuracy, and what did choosing it cost you?","stem":"","lookFor":"Names a version as the best balance and states the cost explicitly - what was given up on the other goal - showing the student expects trade-offs rather than claiming every goal improved at no cost.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Add a third goal, tone, and describe in a few lines how balancing three goals is harder than balancing two, using your log.","stem":"","lookFor":"Adds tone as a third goal and explains, with reference to log evidence, why more goals means more competing costs and a harder choice of best balance.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]}]}},"g6-bridging-w3-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core","number":5,"week":3,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"How Data Shapes a Solution's Outcome","summary":"Students trace how the examples a fruit-ripeness sorter learned from decide the answer it gives, then predict how changing that data would move the outcome.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CE"],"objectives":["Explain how the data used changes the outcome of a given solution.","Predict how a change in the data would change the solution's result."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Two apps can share the exact same code and still disagree about the same photo. The secret is not in the code it is in what each app was shown. Today you become a data detective, tracing how the examples an app learns from decide the answer it gives."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can explain, in my own words, how the data fed into a solution shapes the outcome it produces.","I can predict what would happen to the outcome if the data were changed, and give a reason.","I can point to a specific part of the data that most affects the result."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"solution","definition":"The whole system that solves a problem - the data plus the model built from it."},{"term":"data","definition":"The examples a solution learns from."},{"term":"outcome","definition":"The answer or result the solution gives."},{"term":"pattern","definition":"The regularity a model finds across many examples."},{"term":"balance","definition":"Whether the data has roughly fair amounts of each category."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"A solution is data plus a model","body":["When people say an app 'decides', they often picture a programmer writing every rule by hand. Machine-learning solutions do not work that way. They build a **model** by studying [[data]] - lots of examples - and capturing the [[pattern]]s inside.","Because the pattern comes from the examples, the examples quietly control the answer. Feed the model different data and it learns a different pattern, so it gives a different [[outcome]], even with the very same code."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Amount and balance both matter","body":["Data influences an outcome in two ways. First, the *amount*: if the model saw 100 sunny photos and only 3 rainy ones, it treats sunny as normal and stumbles on rain.","Second, the [[balance]]: adding more of what the model already has plenty of changes little, but adding examples of a rare or missing category can move the outcome a lot. A data detective looks for the gap."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Your teacher will run a fruit-ripeness sorter on the board. Your job is not to run it - it is to trace why it answers the way it does, using the data list on your card."],"items":["Read the data list on your card and say what the sorter mostly saw.","Watch one fruit go through and note the outcome.","Draw an arrow from the data batch you think caused that outcome.","Write: 'The outcome is ___ because the data mostly showed ___.'","Check your reason against a second fruit - does the story still hold?"],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which data batch had the biggest effect on the outcome, and how do you know?","If you could add only one batch of new data, which would you add to make the sorter fairer?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one sentence explaining how data shaped today's outcome.","Note one thing you would check before trusting a solution's answer."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 3 - Core - Worksheet | How Data Shapes a Solution's Outcome","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Trace the data: use the worked solution card. Draw an arrow from the data batch to the outcome, then explain the link in one sentence.","stem":"The outcome is ___\nbecause the data mostly showed ___","lookFor":"A named data feature causing the outcome - for example that the training data was mostly ripe brown dates, so the sorter treats brown as the normal ripe look - rather than a general 'the data did it'.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"What if the data changed? Predict how the outcome changes if we add 50 green unripe date photos. Point to the exact data that causes your predicted change.","stem":"I predict the outcome will ___\nbecause the added data ___","lookFor":"Reasoning that links the added category to the shifted outcome - more green dates labelled 'unripe' correctly because the sorter now has examples of a category it was missing. A plausible shift with only vague justification is partial.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Data change log: for each data change, record what changed and how the outcome moved. Use the columns Data change | Predicted outcome | Which data caused it | Confidence (high/med/low).","lookFor":"Each row attributes the predicted movement to a specific data batch rather than to luck or to the code, and the confidence rating is used honestly on the rows where the data link is weaker.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"process"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: explain in two sentences how the data shaped the sorter's outcome, and predict one data change that would flip it.","lookFor":"A clear path traced from specific data to the outcome, plus a prediction of the shift justified by pointing to the exact data changed. Attributing the outcome to luck, the code or something being broken is not yet there.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): design the smallest possible data change that would flip the sorter from 'ripe' to 'unripe' on a brown date, and explain why so small a change can have such a big effect.","lookFor":"The gap-filling idea: a small batch that supplies a category the sorter barely had moves the outcome more than adding to a category it already has plenty of. Look for the smallest change named precisely, not just 'add more data'.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-bridging-w3-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab","number":6,"week":3,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Trace-the-Data Investigation (Lab)","summary":"Students run three controlled single data swaps on the ripeness sorter, log the before-and-after outcomes, and use that evidence to identify and verify the most influential change.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CE"],"objectives":["Change the data in a worked solution and observe and record the effect on the outcome.","Identify which single data change most alters the outcome, and explain why."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["In the Core lesson you predicted how a data change would move the outcome. A prediction is only a guess until you test it. Today you run a fair test: change one thing, watch the outcome, and let the evidence tell you which data matters most."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run a controlled data change and record the outcome before and after.","I can compare data changes and identify which one moved the outcome the most.","I can explain, using my recorded evidence, why that change had the biggest effect."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"controlled change","definition":"Changing one thing at a time so you know what caused the result."},{"term":"before/after","definition":"The outcome measured just before and just after a single change."},{"term":"shift","definition":"How far the outcome moved because of a change."},{"term":"influential","definition":"Having a large effect on the outcome."},{"term":"verify","definition":"To re-test and confirm an effect is real, not a one-off."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Change one thing at a time","body":["To find out how much a data batch matters, you must isolate it. Measure the outcome **before**, change only that one batch, and measure again. The difference is the influence of that batch.","If you change two batches at once and the outcome moves, you cannot tell which batch did it. A fair test changes one variable at a time - the same rule scientists use in any experiment."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Evidence beats opinion","body":["Your log is your evidence. Without a written before-and-after, you are relying on memory, and memory blurs across many swaps.","Once you have run several swaps, the most [[influential]] batch is the one with the biggest [[shift]]. [[Verify]] it by re-running on a fresh fruit - if the effect repeats, you have proof, not a coincidence."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Work in threes with roles: swapper, recorder, checker. You will run three single data swaps, log each outcome, and find the most influential change."],"items":["Write your Core prediction at the top of the log.","Record the BEFORE outcome for your test fruit.","Swap in one data batch and record the AFTER outcome.","Note the shift: none, a little, or a lot.","Repeat for two more single swaps.","Rank the swaps and star the most influential.","Verify the winner on a fresh fruit and write your conclusion."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Did the evidence match your prediction? Where did it surprise you?","Why might a small batch beat a large one for influence?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write your evidence-based conclusion about the most influential data change.","Note one thing a fair test taught you that a guess could not."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 3 - Lab - Worksheet | Trace-the-Data Investigation (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Run three single swaps: for each swap, record the before outcome, the after outcome, and the size of the shift. Change only one batch at a time.","stem":"Before: ___\nAfter: ___\nShift: ___","lookFor":"One batch changed at a time with a clean before value recorded before the swap, not filled in afterwards. Changing several batches at once means the outcome cannot be attributed and the swap should be redone.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"process"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Name and verify the winner: state which swap was most influential, justify it from your log, and describe how you verified it on a fresh fruit.","stem":"The most influential swap was ___\nMy log shows ___\nI verified it by ___","lookFor":"The winner is the batch that filled the biggest data gap - typically the green-date batch - justified from the logged before/after shifts rather than opinion, and confirmed by a re-test on a fresh fruit that shows the effect repeats.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Investigation log: log every single swap and its effect so you can rank them fairly. Use the columns Swap (one batch) | Before outcome | After outcome | Shift size | Rank.","lookFor":"Every row records one batch only, with both before and after outcomes filled in and a shift size (none / a little / a lot). A missing before value or a muddled swap makes the ranking unreliable.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"process"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: state the most influential data change you found and give the recorded evidence that proves it.","lookFor":"Evidence, not opinion: the named swap is backed by the before/after shift written in the log, and ideally by the repeat result from the verification run.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): design a data swap you predict will produce zero outcome change, then test it. Explain why some data has no influence on this solution.","lookFor":"A swap that only adds more of what the sorter already has plenty of - for example another brown-date batch - with the explanation that it repeats a pattern the model already learned, so it fills no gap and moves nothing.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-bridging-w4-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core","number":7,"week":4,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"Misinformation Risks and Verification","summary":"Students learn why fluent AI text is not the same as true text, then name the exact claim at risk in a curated output and pair it with a concrete check against a trusted source.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["GE"],"objectives":["Explain how an AI output could spread misinformation.","Propose at least one concrete verification strategy and explain why it helps."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Misinformation Risks and Verification","body":["An AI can write an answer that sounds completely sure and is completely wrong. It is built to sound right, not to be right. Today you learn to spot the exact risk in an output and pair it with a check that makes it trustworthy before you ever share it."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can explain a specific way an AI output could be wrong and spread misinformation.","I can propose a concrete verification step and say why it makes the claim more trustworthy.","I can move from 'it might be wrong' to naming the exact risk and the exact check."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"misinformation","definition":"False information that spreads, whether or not anyone meant to deceive."},{"term":"claim","definition":"A specific statement that could be true or false, like a number or a date."},{"term":"verify","definition":"To confirm a claim against a trusted, independent source."},{"term":"trusted source","definition":"A source kept accurate by people who check facts, like an official website."},{"term":"layered check","definition":"Verifying with more than one independent source for extra reliability."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Why fluent is not the same as true","body":["An AI language system predicts the next likely word, over and over, to build fluent text. It is optimised to sound plausible, not to check facts, so it can state a wrong number as confidently as a right one.","That is what makes AI output a [[misinformation]] risk. The output gives no signal for which parts are reliable, so a made-up statistic can look exactly like a real one and spread when people repeat it."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Name the risk, then check it","body":["Good evaluation is specific. Instead of 'this might be wrong', say exactly what is at risk: the number, the name, the date, or the source.","Then pair that risk with a concrete check against a [[trusted source]] that is independent of the AI. If you can, layer two sources - one source can be wrong too."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will study three curated AI outputs. For each, name the exact risk and propose a concrete check. Your teacher will verify one live against a trusted source."],"items":["Read each output and underline the exact claim you doubt.","Name the risk type: number, date, name, or source.","Choose a trusted source that could settle it.","Write the check and why it helps.","Watch one live verification and record the verdict."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which output was the hardest to spot a risk in, and why?","Why is verifying before sharing part of using AI responsibly?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the strongest risk-and-verify pair you made today.","Note one habit you will use before sharing any AI answer."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Misinformation Risks and Verification","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"For each curated output, underline the doubtful claim and name the risk type and why it is risky.","stem":"The risky claim is ___\nThe risk type is ___\nIt is risky because ___","lookFor":"Names the exact claim at risk and a risk type - number, date, name or source - with a reason. Model response: the height figure for the landmark could be invented, because the AI predicts plausible numbers and may not have the real one. Reward a named risky element, not a general 'it might be wrong'.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"For one output, write a concrete verification strategy naming a trusted source and explaining why it reduces the risk.","stem":"I would check it by ___\nusing the trusted source ___\nThis helps because ___","lookFor":"The check must target the specific claim and name a trusted source. Model response: check the height on the landmark's official website or a reputable encyclopaedia, because those are maintained by people who verify facts. Look for the independence and reliability reasoning - an official source is independent of the AI and is kept accurate, so agreement makes the claim far more trustworthy.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Record the risk and matching check for each output you evaluated.","stem":"Columns: Output | Risk (specific claim) | Verification step | Trusted source | Why it helps","lookFor":"Mastery: each row pairs the exact claim at risk with a concrete check against a named trusted source and a clear reason it reduces the risk. Partial: a risk is identified but stays general, or the source is vague and the reason thin. Not yet: no workable check, or a check that would not address the risk.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Name one misinformation risk in an AI output and propose one concrete verification strategy, with a reason it helps.","lookFor":"Explains a plausible misinformation risk and proposes a workable verification strategy with a reason. Feedback move: push 'it could be wrong' toward a specific risk plus a specific check; extend confident students to layered verification.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Design a layered verification strategy using two independent trusted sources for one claim, and explain why two sources are more reliable than one.","lookFor":"Stretch target from the pack: designs a layered check using two independent trusted sources and explains why two beats one - a single source can also be wrong or out of date, and two genuinely independent sources rarely share the same error.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]}]}}}};
}

